Consider the following code snippet that is intended to create a null-terminated string and print its contents:

char *foo;
int counter;
foo = calloc(sizeof(char) * 10);
for (counter = 0; counter != 10; counter++) {
    foo[counter] = 'a';
    printf("%s\n", foo);
}

What could be a potential consequence if the loop condition is mistakenly changed to 'counter < 10'?

Review the following pseudo-code for a feature toggle in a web application's settings page.

if (userInput == 'enableFeatureX') {
  displayMessage('Feature X is now enabled.');
}

Given that Feature X is not yet implemented, what type of vulnerability does this code introduce?

Consider the following Java code snippet:

  Long total = 0L;
  for (long j = 0; j < 1000000; j++) {
    total += j;
  }

Which of the following changes could improve the performance of this code?

Consider the following code snippet that attempts to mitigate the vulnerability of misinterpreting input: 

javascript
function validateInput(input) {
  // Intended to prevent misinterpretation of input
  if (/^[0-9]+$/g.test(input)) {
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}


Which alternative implementation would NOT be effective in preventing the misinterpretation of numeric input?

Analyze the following Java code snippet from a web application that logs failed input parsing attempts: 

String val = request.getParameter("val");
try { int value = Integer.parseInt(val); }
catch (NumberFormatException e) { log.info("Failed to parse val = " + val); }

Which vulnerability does this code snippet most likely introduce?

